<SiteLayout title="Safety & boundaries" description="The freedom to meet people needs practical choices about privacy, consent and contact." eyebrow="Respect is the starting point" heading="Connection should respect your boundaries." intro="The freedom to meet people needs practical choices about privacy, consent and contact.">
<section class="editorial-split"><h2>Your choices<br />should carry through.</h2><div><p>We’re designing Swoovr around clear intentions, control over discovery, and the ability to stop unwanted contact. A boundary should apply across the experience, including when someone changes discovery context.</p><p>Blocking, reporting, moderation and a route to challenge decisions are part of the supported pilot requirements. The instructions and support arrangements for each beta will be provided with its invitation.</p><p>We won’t describe a verification check as a guarantee of someone’s intentions or behaviour. <a href="/verification/">Read what verification is intended to mean.</a></p></div></section><section class="editorial-split"><h2>Privacy is part<br />of feeling in control.</h2><div><p>Precise home locations, identity documents and private conversations don’t belong on public profiles. Our approach is to request only the information a feature needs and explain how it is used.</p><p>Private feedback about a connection and a report about concerning conduct need different treatment. We don’t want public attractiveness scores or a popularity contest disguised as community safety.</p><a class="text-link" href="/privacy/">Website privacy notice ↗</a></div></section><section class="reading-section"><h2>Taking a connection offline</h2><p>Keep early plans comfortable for you: choose a public place, arrange your own transport, let someone you trust know your plan and leave whenever you want. You never owe another person a meeting, a message or an explanation for changing your mind.</p><p>Venue participation does not mean a person or meeting has been endorsed as safe. Be cautious about requests for money or pressure to share private information.</p></section><section class="callout"><h2>Something you want<br />the team to know?</h2><p>Use our contact form to raise a website concern, ask about our safety approach, or tell us what you think needs attention. This form is not monitored as an emergency service. If there’s an immediate danger, contact local emergency services.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/?topic=support">Contact the team ↗</a></section>
</SiteLayout>
